sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/somite-number-control--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Temperature test: do clock and elongation Q10s predict meristic plasticity?" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/somite-number-control> ;
    dct:description "Use temperature as a natural perturbation the swarm can exploit from existing data. In ectotherms, temperature changes both clock period and elongation rate. Clock-and-wavefront predicts that total somite number varies with rearing temperature in proportion to the difference of their thermal sensitivities: d ln N / dT ≈ (E_τ − E_elong)/(kT²) in Arrhenius form. If both scale identically, N is temperature-invariant, as reported for zebrafish somitogenesis by Schröter et al. 2008. Compile (a) clock-period and elongation-rate temperature series for zebrafish and any other species with them, and (b) laboratory rearing-temperature experiments reporting vertebral counts in fishes (meristic plasticity literature), plus (c) the latitudinal vertebral-count gradient (Jordan's rule) from FishBase across species and populations. Pre-registered predictions: the measured E difference predicts the sign and magnitude of rearing-temperature effects within a factor of 2, and species whose measured E difference is near zero show no latitudinal gradient." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Agreement or disagreement, within a factor of 2, between d ln N/dT predicted from measured clock and elongation thermal sensitivities and the observed rearing-temperature effect on vertebral counts. A sign mismatch in two or more species refutes the clock-and-wavefront account of meristic plasticity." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: No species has both a clock-temperature series and a rearing-temperature vertebral-count experiment, so predictions and observations come from different species and the test is indirect." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register the Arrhenius prediction and tolerance. 2. Extract temperature series for τ and elongation from published studies; estimate E values with CIs. 3. Compile rearing-temperature vertebral-count experiments; compute d ln N/dT per study. 4. Compare predicted and observed slopes; run a phylogenetically controlled test of Jordan's rule against predicted sensitivity where data allow." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Matched clock and meristic data for the same species are rare; new temperature-series time-lapse experiments would make the test decisive." ;
    skos:note "Tool: comparative-data" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:note "Tool: organisms-layer" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch17-04> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.fishbase.se/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1002/dvdy.21458> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07020> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/somite-number-control> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "What sets vertebrate somite number?" .

  <https://www.fishbase.se/> dct:title "FishBase" ;
    dct:description "Vertebral counts and distribution latitudes" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1002/dvdy.21458> dct:title "Dynamics of zebrafish somitogenesis" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Schröter et al. 2008, Dynamics of zebrafish somitogenesis (temperature dependence)" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07020> dct:title "Control of segment number in vertebrate embryos" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gomez et al. 2008" .
}

